#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124418 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebec626a-be3d-5920-9588-48f3df108944" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Two-Variable Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "a7899e04-8be9-597a-a9b6-111201ebad2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8607" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y) = 9 - x^2 - y^2$ on the line segment $\\overline {AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0, 0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle 2, 1\\rangle,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c$ such that\n\n$$\nf (\\mathbf b) − f (\\mathbf a) = \\nabla f (\\mathbf c) · (\\mathbf b − \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x, y) = 9 - x^2 - y^2$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf a =  \\langle 0, 0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle 2, 1\\rangle$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,1)-f(0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,1⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\ (9-{2}^{2}-{1}^{2})-(9-{0}^{2}-{0}^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ -5 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\nabla f$ and evaluating at $\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1, c_2\\rangle,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-2x,-2y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-2{c}_{1},-2{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ -5 & =⟨-2{c}_{1},-2{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ -5 & =-4{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2} \\\\ 5 & =4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-0}{1-0} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1,c_2\\rangle$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $4c_1 + 2c_2 = 5,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{c}_{1}+2(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}) & =5 \\\\ 4{c}_{1}+{c}_{1} & =5 \\\\ 5{c}_{1} & =5 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find $c_2 = \\dfrac{c_1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $\\left(1, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124419 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124607 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f9ea905-6eb5-55fe-b487-b115406bbc46" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Three-Variable Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "2b224972-6a43-5691-9e46-abff577311d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8608" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z) = x^2 + y - z^2$ on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,0,1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle 2,3,0\\rangle,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y, z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c$ such that\n\n$$\nf (\\mathbf b) − f (\\mathbf a) = \\nabla f (\\mathbf c) · (\\mathbf b − \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x, y,z) = x^2 + y - z^2$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,0,1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b= \\langle 2,3,0\\rangle$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,3,0)-f(0,0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,3,0⟩-⟨0,0,1⟩) \\\\ 7-(-1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ 8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\nabla f$ and evaluating at $\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1, c_2, c_3\\rangle,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,1,-2z⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1},1,-2{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ 8 & =⟨2{c}_{1},1,-2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ 8 & =4{c}_{1}+3+2{c}_{3} \\\\ 5 & =4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{y}{3}=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx = 2t , \\quad y= 3t, \\quad z = 1-t, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf c= \\langle c_1, c_2, c_3\\rangle$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\nc_1 = 2t , \\quad c_2 = 3t , \\quad c_3 = 1-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $4c_1 + 2c_3 = 5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2t)+2(1-t) & =5 \\\\ 8t+2-2t & =5 \\\\ 6t & =3 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of $c_1,$ $c_2,$ and $c_3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2(\\frac{1}{2})=1, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $\\left(1, \\dfrac{3}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124423 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124611 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45abb029-42f5-5297-b793-f893178e0c29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation Implied by the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "0368b61d-2d85-58af-9af0-ec09edf877b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8693" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ where $\\mathbf a=\\langle 5,3\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b=\\langle 4,2\\rangle$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(\\mathbf a)= 5$ and $f(\\mathbf b)=6,$ then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c?$\n\nI. $f_x(\\mathbf c) + f_y(\\mathbf c) = -1$\n\nII. $f_x(\\mathbf c) + f_y(\\mathbf c) = 1$\n\nIII. $f_x(\\mathbf c) +f_y(\\mathbf c) = 2$" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c$ such that\n\n$$\nf (\\mathbf b) − f (\\mathbf a) = \\nabla f (\\mathbf c) · (\\mathbf b − \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)·(b-a) \\\\ 6-5 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨4,2⟩-⟨5,3⟩) \\\\ 1 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 1 & =-{f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\nf_x(\\mathbf c) + f_y(\\mathbf c) =-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124611 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45abb029-42f5-5297-b793-f893178e0c29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation Implied by the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52d2c808-6a32-5acf-8e51-89148e82ede9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)-{f}_{z}(c)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565059} {:answer/id #uuid "852545d0-96ef-508b-83ae-f36cad069693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)-{f}_{z}(c)=-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565147} {:answer/id #uuid "b3fb31b9-bbda-5e8c-9ef4-be73347fe4f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)-3{f}_{z}(c)=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565208} {:answer/id #uuid "d1be7867-3292-5dc2-8849-8f2ba035929f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{f}_{x}(c)+4{f}_{y}(c)-5{f}_{z}(c)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565248} {:answer/id #uuid "e7e12a8a-8a82-50af-b9be-2d1afca28f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)-{f}_{z}(c)=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52d2c808-6a32-5acf-8e51-89148e82ede9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)-{f}_{z}(c)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565059} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e4d565f-6191-5133-ae29-4a063b9a73e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14bd3999-4f71-5f69-b9ed-e9c986ba9f80" :question/math-academy-id "q-105766" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨1,0,4⟩$ and $b=⟨2,-1,3⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=5$ and $f(b)=8,$ then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\text{_______________}\\,.$ *" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ 8-5 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨2,-1,3⟩-⟨1,0,4⟩) \\\\ 3 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 3 & ={f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)-{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that ${f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)-{f}_{z}(c)=3.$ *"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124611 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45abb029-42f5-5297-b793-f893178e0c29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation Implied by the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2681b92b-2fe3-55de-90b4-7056cbf32e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)-4{f}_{z}(c)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564998} {:answer/id #uuid "392f4eb9-7e9a-598f-bfef-2848170a47cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{f}_{x}(c)-9{f}_{z}(c)=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565021} {:answer/id #uuid "82ece195-67e9-55ba-a35c-58754eb63f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)-4{f}_{y}(c)-9{f}_{z}(c)=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565142} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf20891-6146-5bf6-ba31-0c4e04a83100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{f}_{x}(c)+9{f}_{z}(c)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565244} {:answer/id #uuid "f5171abd-cddd-53a2-8365-f3f4701ed218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{f}_{y}(c)-6{f}_{z}(c)=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "392f4eb9-7e9a-598f-bfef-2848170a47cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{f}_{x}(c)-9{f}_{z}(c)=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565021} :answer-field/id #uuid "2660f5ee-d91a-5b88-baed-9e30b77e6148" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf45d7d2-b32a-596a-b136-67ab888373db" :question/math-academy-id "q-105767" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨-1,-2,4⟩$ and $b=⟨3,-2,-5⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=-3$ and $f(b)=4,$ then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\text{_______________}\\,.$ *" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ 4-(-3) & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨3,-2,-5⟩-⟨-1,-2,4⟩) \\\\ 7 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨4,0,-9⟩ \\\\ 7 & =4{f}_{x}(c)-9{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $4{f}_{x}(c)-9{f}_{z}(c)=7.$ *"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebec626a-be3d-5920-9588-48f3df108944" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Two-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "162b92da-df23-5c30-ad49-2fd32f33a03e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564972} {:answer/id #uuid "295bfcf1-2a63-541e-8767-7a6693f4baa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565006} {:answer/id #uuid "724aaf30-aac6-5e20-acb1-5fa47f134fe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565113} {:answer/id #uuid "8053c7ab-909b-5f33-9ce9-0d798cb23006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565136} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb4def9-72b8-5730-ab1a-af5ca0f9d64a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{4},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8053c7ab-909b-5f33-9ce9-0d798cb23006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565136} :answer-field/id #uuid "59557dca-7f7b-53f1-95d6-4b59fe6e4251" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c3d7252-78a8-50a4-b514-2643c3b47ad3" :question/math-academy-id "q-106075" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,3)-f(0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,3⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\ ({1}^{2}+3)-({0}^{2}+1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ 3 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,1⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ 3 & =⟨2{c}_{1},1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ 3 & =2{c}_{1}+2 \\\\ 1 & =2{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{3-1} & =\\frac{x-0}{1-0} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{2} & =x \\\\ y & =2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =2{c}_{1}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2},$ we obtain that ${c}_{2}=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1=2.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{1}{2},2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124607 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f9ea905-6eb5-55fe-b487-b115406bbc46" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Three-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07f8a323-c0be-535b-948e-c78114672e3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3})$ and $(1,1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564953} {:answer/id #uuid "50cf239c-b6f5-566d-958b-b55860061ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565053} {:answer/id #uuid "c298df37-cd11-54c8-a6ae-8244b42ec27a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(1,1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565233} {:answer/id #uuid "c92734c5-3437-5572-a60d-19e95c9b9d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565242} {:answer/id #uuid "e1cfcf18-3023-5d0a-9e55-6fde879778df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50cf239c-b6f5-566d-958b-b55860061ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565053} :answer-field/id #uuid "568fe48b-b287-53d7-8a0d-1c40859c56fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a75a7fb-927d-5de3-ad94-97e90ac6e83d" :question/math-academy-id "q-106583" :question/problem "Find the points that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}-yz$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨1,1,1⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}-yz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨1,1,1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,1)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,1,1⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 0-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨3{x}^{2},-z,-y⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨3{c}_{1}^{2},-{c}_{3},-{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =⟨3{c}_{1}^{2},-{c}_{3},-{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =3{c}_{1}^{2}-{c}_{3}-{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-0}{1}=\\frac{z-0}{1} \\\\ x & =y=z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=t,\\,z=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t,\\,{c}_{2}=t,\\,{c}_{3}=t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $3{c}_{1}^{2}-{c}_{3}-{c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-t-t & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-2t & =0 \\\\ t(3t-2) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & ={c}_{2}={c}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $(0,0,0).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{2}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & ={c}_{2}={c}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebec626a-be3d-5920-9588-48f3df108944" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Two-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "009b6ee6-cb03-533c-b05a-a8d696a75880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564939} {:answer/id #uuid "3698d5a3-a25a-593a-975b-351b9af63bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{5}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565019} {:answer/id #uuid "78dc3ae4-aaae-53b9-a44a-1ae2307a7917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{8}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565121} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb94ca4-f440-5612-bb29-2301fce49975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{7}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565156} {:answer/id #uuid "9efd85af-9e07-5549-b93f-6870a2b05040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{5}{6},\\frac{7}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "009b6ee6-cb03-533c-b05a-a8d696a75880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564939} :answer-field/id #uuid "0843a760-e941-5346-bbf2-95f564f94674" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e5b6cf6-ccdb-5467-84b0-18bae4498b37" :question/math-academy-id "q-36834" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for \n $f(x,y)={y}^{3}-{x}^{3}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨-1,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,2⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)={y}^{3}-{x}^{3}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨-1,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,2⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2)-f(-1,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨0,2⟩-⟨-1,1⟩) \\\\ ({2}^{3}-{0}^{3})-({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ 6 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-3{x}^{2},3{y}^{2}⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-3{c}_{1}^{2},3{c}_{2}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ 6 & =⟨-3{c}_{1}^{2},3{c}_{2}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ 6 & =-3{c}_{1}^{2}+3{c}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =-{c}_{1}^{2}+{c}_{2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{2-1} & =\\frac{x+1}{0+1} \\\\ y-1 & =x+1 \\\\ y & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & ={c}_{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $-{c}_{1}^{2}+{c}_{2}^{2}=2$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({c}_{2}-2)^{2}+{c}_{2}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 4{c}_{2}-4 & =2 \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{1}={c}_{2}-2=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124607 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f9ea905-6eb5-55fe-b487-b115406bbc46" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Three-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d15fa8a-7adb-517a-a824-c1cc2486e352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564982} {:answer/id #uuid "1d6bd118-2348-5c1b-9b4e-580c8ff5121b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564984} {:answer/id #uuid "55f88006-7ccf-567b-a57c-fe1c317bd9da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},2,\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565066} {:answer/id #uuid "e256dd70-be6c-5fd9-94cd-963d6af2d61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565274} {:answer/id #uuid "fe14b2e0-69de-5fbe-9a68-76ef240bc41a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},2,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe14b2e0-69de-5fbe-9a68-76ef240bc41a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},2,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565325} :answer-field/id #uuid "64d36f39-4452-5dfc-9eca-123cea93a660" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da56330d-fd66-56f8-bb18-6db981b4ba6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98118" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)=4xz-{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,1,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3,2⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=4xz-{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3,2⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,3,2)-f(0,1,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,3,2⟩-⟨0,1,1⟩) \\\\ 3-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ 3 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4z,-2y,4x+2z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{3},-2{c}_{2},4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ 3 & =⟨4{c}_{3},-2{c}_{2},4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ 3 & =4{c}_{3}-4{c}_{2}+4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3} \\\\ 3 & =4{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2}+6{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{1} & =\\frac{y-1}{2}=\\frac{z-1}{1} \\\\ x & =\\frac{y-1}{2}=z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=2t+1,\\,z=t+1,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t,\\,{c}_{2}=2t+1,\\,{c}_{3}=t+1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $3=4{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2}+6{c}_{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4t-4(2t+1)+6(t+1) & =3 \\\\ 4t-8t-4+6t+6 & =3 \\\\ 2t & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2(\\frac{1}{2})+1=2, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(\\frac{1}{2},2,\\frac{3}{2}).$"}]}
